{"ex-502-1":{"number":"1","title":"Identifying isotopes","question":"<p>Three different nuclides are shown below.</p><p><sub>20</sub><sup>40</sup><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> <sub>20</sub><sup>46</sup><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> <sub>22</sub><sup>46</sup><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>(a) Find the numbers of protons and neutrons in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Which two nuclides are isotopes of the same element? Explain briefly.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Number of protons = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 20</p><p>Number of neutrons = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> − <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 46 − 20 = 26</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> have the same atomic number (20) but different mass numbers (40 and 46). Therefore, they are isotopes of the same element.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 5  |  Chapter 502  |  Source PDF p. 6","source":{"name":"502 Examples.docx","sha256":"1701f757efdee4e60dd8a3b27b91f8e44038050e90dc9600e7a4d8c2e89b0477","block":2},"id":"ex-502-1","chapter":"502"},"ex-502-2":{"number":"2","title":"An α decay of radium-226","question":"<p>Radium-226 (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Ra</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>) decays into radon (Rn) by emitting an α particle. Write down an equation for the decay process.</p>","answer":"<p>The α decay can be written as:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Ra</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> → <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Rn</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>He</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>Consider the mass numbers and atomic numbers before and after the decay.</p><p>226 = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + 4 88 = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + 2</p><p>and</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 222 <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 86</p><p>Therefore, the equation is</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Ra</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> → <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Rn</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>He</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 5  |  Chapter 502  |  Source PDF p. 10","source":{"name":"502 Examples.docx","sha256":"1701f757efdee4e60dd8a3b27b91f8e44038050e90dc9600e7a4d8c2e89b0477","block":12},"id":"ex-502-2","chapter":"502"},"ex-502-3":{"number":"3","title":"Decay series of uranium-235","question":"<p>The decay series of uranium-235 is shown below.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/94c23423c21fc91d96f09002.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:347.9px\"/></p><p>(a) What kind of particle is emitted in each decay shown above?</p><p>(b) The decay series ends with a stable lead-207 (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Pb</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>). How many α particles and β particles are emitted in the complete series?</p><p>(c) From the above figure, can you deduce whether γ radiation is emitted in the decay series? Explain your answer.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>U</mtext></mrow><mover><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>→</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>α</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mover><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Th</mtext></mrow><mover><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>→</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>β</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mover><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Pa</mtext></mrow><mover><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>→</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>α</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mover><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Ac</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>→</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>⋯</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>(b) Number of α particles emitted</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>change in mass number</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>mass number of α particle</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>The decay series can be written as:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>U</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>→</mtext></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Pb</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>He</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>Consider the atomic numbers.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>Four β particles are emitted in the complete series.</p><p>(c) No. γ emission does not involve any change in the atomic number and the mass number. Therefore, it cannot be shown on the graph.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The mass number changes in an α decay, but not in a β decay or γ emission.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 5  |  Chapter 502  |  Source PDF p. 13","source":{"name":"502 Examples.docx","sha256":"1701f757efdee4e60dd8a3b27b91f8e44038050e90dc9600e7a4d8c2e89b0477","block":24},"id":"ex-502-3","chapter":"502"},"ex-502-4":{"number":"4","title":"Finding half-life from count rate","question":"<p>A GM counter is used to measure the count rate of a sodium-24 sample over a period of time. The graph below shows how the count rate varies with time. Sodium-24 will decay to form a stable product.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/11dd19f7ac089b869b22ce57.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:445.0px\"/></p><p>(a) Why does the graph flatten out rather than fall to zero after some time?</p><p>(b) Estimate the half-life of sodium-24 from the graph.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) In that period, the activity of the sample falls to almost zero. The count rate measured by the GM counter is due to background radiation.</p><p>(b) Count rate due to background radiation = 40 counts per min</p><p>Initial count rate due to sample = 400 − 40 = 360 counts per min</p><p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>½</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>,</p><p>measured count rate = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + 40 = 220 counts per min</p><p>From the graph,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>½</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 15 h</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 5  |  Chapter 502  |  Source PDF p. 19","source":{"name":"502 Examples.docx","sha256":"1701f757efdee4e60dd8a3b27b91f8e44038050e90dc9600e7a4d8c2e89b0477","block":47},"id":"ex-502-4","chapter":"502"},"ex-502-5":{"number":"5","title":"Activity and half-life","question":"<p>Sodium-24 will decay to form a stable product. It has a decay constant of</p><p>1.28 × 10<sup>−5</sup> s<sup>−1</sup> and a half-life of 15 hours. A sample of sodium-24 has an</p><p>activity of 8 × 10<sup>5</sup> Bq.</p><p>(a) Find the number of undecayed nuclei in the sample.</p><p>(b) What will be the activity of the sample after 45 hours?</p><p>(c) Find the time needed for the activity to decrease to 2.5 × 10<sup>4</sup> Bq.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math></p><p>(b) Number of half-lives passed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Bq</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Bq</mtext></mrow><mover><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>→</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mover><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Bq</mtext></mrow><mover><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>→</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><m