w></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 6.93 × 10<sup>5</sup> s</p><p>(b) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>e<sup>−kt</sup>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = e<sup>−kt</sup></p><p>ln (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>) = ln e<sup>−kt</sup></p><p>ln (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>) = −<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = – <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> ln (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>) = – <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> ln (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>) = 5.99 × 10<sup>6</sup> s</p><p>∴ It takes 5.99 × 10<sup>6</sup> s for the activity to decrease to 1 kBq.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Skill</b></p><p><b>Mathematics</b></p><p>Solving exponential equations with an unknown index p.121</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 5  |  Chapter 502  |  Source PDF p. 24","source":{"name":"502 Examples.docx","sha256":"1701f757efdee4e60dd8a3b27b91f8e44038050e90dc9600e7a4d8c2e89b0477","block":78},"id":"ex-502-6","chapter":"502"},"ex-502-7":{"number":"7","title":"Decay of radium-226","question":"<p>Radium-226 (Ra-226) decays to radon (Rn) by emitting an α particle and has a half-life of 1600 years. The radon is also radioactive and in turn decays into other radionuclides. The process carries on until a stable lead (Pb) nuclide is formed.</p><p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Pb</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Pb</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Pb</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are three stable isotopes of lead. Determine which isotope(s) could be the nuclide formed in the above decay series.</p><p>(b) A sample contains 1 mg of Ra-226.</p><p>(i) Show that the mass of undecayed Ra-226 will be about 0.98 mg after 50 years.</p><p>(ii) Simon says, ‘The mass of the sample will be reduced to 0.98 mg after 50 years.’ Is he correct? Explain.</p><p>(iii) The corrected count rate of the sample will not be 98% of the original value after 50 years. Explain the reason.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) The change in mass number in a decay series must be a multiple of 4.</p><p>∴ Only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>Pb</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> can be the nuclide formed in the decay series.</p><p>(b) (i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>½</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 4.33 × 10<sup>−4</sup> yr<sup>−1</sup></p><p>Since mass of undecayed nuclei <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ∝ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>e<sup>−kt</sup>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>mg</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>≈</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>mg</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>o</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>:</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>Since mass of undecayed nuclei <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>∝</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math></p><p>= 0.979 mg ≈ 0.98 mg</p><p>(ii) He is wrong.</p><p>Although 0.02 mg of Ra-226 decay in 50 years, the decayed nuclei do not disappear but instead change into other nuclei which also make up the mass of the sample.</p><p>(iii) Most products in the decay series, including radon, are radioactive and contribute to the measured count rate.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The mass number is reduced by 4 during an α decay and remains unchanged during a β decay.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Year and year<sup>−1</sup> (yr<sup>−1</sup>) are used as the units of t and k respectively.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>In applying this equation, the number of half-lives n needs not be an integer and can be</p><p>expressed as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>½</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 5  |  Chapter 502  |  Source PDF p. 25","source":{"name":"502 Examples.docx","sha256":"1701f757efdee4e60dd8a3b27b91f8e44038050e90dc9600e7a4d8c2e89b0477","block":95},"id":"ex-502-7","chapter":"502"},"ex-502-8":{"number":"8","title":"Filling bottles of juice","question":"<p>Fruit juices contained in plastic bottles are made in a factory. A radioactive source is placed on one side of a conveyor belt and a detector is placed at the same level on the other side. They are used to monitor whether the juice reaches the required level in a bottle.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/3bc483617a89eb0d5144c0fd.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:423.7px\"/></p><p>(a) Explain how this system can detect the bottles not filled up to the required level.</p><p>(b) Which type of radioactive source (α, β or γ ) should be used? Explain why the other two are not suitable.</p><p>(c) Two sources emitting the type of radiation in (b) are available. Their half-lives are 20 minutes and 4 years. Which source should be used? Explain why the other one is not suitable.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Some radiation will be absorbed by the juice if the juice reaches the required level in a bottle. Therefore, if a bottle is not filled up to the required level, more radiation will pass through the bottle and the counter will record a higher count rate.</p><p>(b) A β source should be used.</p><p>An α source is not suitable because α radiation cannot pass through the bottle.</p><p>A γ source is not suitable because the effect of the juice on the penetration of γ radiation is negligible.</p><p>(c) The source with a half-life of 4 years should be used.</p><p>The source with a half-life of 20 minutes cannot maintain a stable activity for a reasonable period of time (e.g. several months).</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 5  |  Chapter 502  |  Source PDF p. 31","source":{"name":"502 Examples.docx","sha256":"1701f757efdee4e60dd8a3b27b91f8e44038050e90dc9600e7a4d8c2e89b0477","block":125},"id":"ex-502-8","chapter":"502"},"ex-502-9":{"number":"9","title":"Carbon-14 dating","question":"<p>In an archaeological site, a sample of charcoal containing 0.5 kg of carbon is found and it came from a plant which died a long time ago. The ratio of mass of carbon-14 to all carbon nuclei in a living plant is 1.6 × 10<sup>−12</sup>. The half-life of C-14 is 5730 years. The molar mass of C-14 is 14 g.</p><p>Given: 1 year = 3.16 × 10<sup>7</sup> s, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 6.02 × 10<sup>23</sup> mol<sup>−1</sup></p><p>(a) A sample from a living plant contains 0.5 kg of carbon. Estimate the mass of C-14 in the sample.</p><p>(b) Estimate the activity of the sample in (a).</p><p>(c) The activity of the sample of charcoal is 11.8 Bq. Estimate the age of this sample in years.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Mass of C-14 in living sample = 0.5 × (1.6 × 10<sup>−12</sup>) = 8 × 10<sup>−13</sup> kg</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>½</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 3.828 × 10<sup>−12</sup> s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>Number of mole <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>total mass</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>molar mass</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 5.71 × 10<sup>−11</sup> mol</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = (5.71 × 10<sup>−11</sup>) × (6.02 × 10<sup>23</sup>) = 3.44 × 10<sup>13</sup></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = (3.828 × 10<sup>−12</sup>) × (3.44 × 10<sup>13</sup>) = 131.7 Bq ≈ 132 Bq</p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>½</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.21 × 10<sup>−4</sup> yr<sup>−1</sup></p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>e<sup>−kt</sup>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = – <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> ln (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>) = – <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> ln (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>) = 19 900 yr</p><p>∴ The age of this sample is 19 900 yr.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>See the steps in Example 6 on p.66.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>When k is in yr<sup>−1</sup>, t is in yr.</p>","think":"<p>Why can’t carbon-14 dating be used to determine the age of remains which are over 1 million years old?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>One million years is about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> half-lives. The remaining fraction of C-14 is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Its activity is far too small to distinguish from background and contamination, so the age cannot be measured reliably by C-14 dating.</p>","location":"Book 5  |  Chapter 502  |  Source PDF p. 33","source":{"name":"502 Examples.docx","sha256":"1701f757efdee4e60dd8a3b27b91f8e44038050e90dc9600e7a4d8c2e89b0477","block":139},"id":"ex-502-9","chapter":"502"}}